  "raw_text": "Step 1: Multiply both sides of the equation by the least common denominator (LCD) of the fractions, which is $(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4)$ to clear the denominators.\n\nStep 2: Simplify the resulting equation:\n\\[1(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) + 2(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4) + 3(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 4) + 4(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3) = (2010x - 4)(x^2 - 1)(x^2 - 2)(x^2 - 3)(x^2 - 4).\\]\n\nStep 3: Expand and simplify the left side of the equation. However, instead of expanding fully, we can observe that the polynomial on the left side will have roots at $x = \\pm 1, \\pm \\sqrt{2}, \\pm \\sqrt{3}, \\pm 2$ because these values make one of the denominators zero, thus making the corresponding term undefined or zeroing out the fraction.\n\nStep 4: The right side of the equation is a polynomial of degree 5, and since the left side has roots at $x = \\pm 1, \\pm \\sqrt{2}, \\pm \\sqrt{3}, \\pm 2$, these are also roots of the entire equation. Therefore, the sum of all complex solutions is the sum of these roots.\n\nStep 5: Calculate the sum of the roots:\n\\[1 + (-1) + \\sqrt{2} + (-\\sqrt{2}) + \\sqrt{3} + (-\\sqrt{3}) + 2 + (-2) = 0.\\]\n\nFinal answer: $\\boxed{0}$<|im_end|>",
  "reforward_peak_bytes": 15485350400,
